      "math_academy_id": "q-108147",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/e59a35140e008485fc092f716c318ff1ade67b68a8b9988150308af576759466.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,3)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,3)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-1$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $1$ unit *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,2).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/56c48b2115d50b1d4e6ab105ba8214275a43efe0267857370976df55a205252a.png)",
